endobj
435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 767 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a sound sensor is connected to analog pin A3 \(SOUND_PIN\) and a TM1637 4-digit display is connected to pins 8 \(CLK_PIN\) and 9 \(DIO_PIN\). The display is used to show the sound level in approximate decibels \(dB\).)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 768 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It begins serial communication using Serial.begin\(9600\) and initializes the display using setBrightness\(\) and clear\(\).)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 769 0 R
/E (A separate function called readDb\(\) is used to calculate the sound level. It reads multiple samples from the sound sensor for a short time and finds the maximum and minimum values. The difference \(peak-to-peak value\) is converted into voltage.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 770 0 R
/E (Then, using a logarithmic formula, the voltage is converted into an approximate dB value. A calibration offset is added, and the value is limited between 0 and 99.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 771 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It calls readDb\(\) to get the sound level, prints it on the Serial Monitor, and displays it on the TM1637 display using showNumberDec\(\).)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (So overall, the Arduino measures sound intensity, converts it into dB, and shows it on a digital display.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
